===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|neologism}} A [[subgenre]] of [[speculative]] [[science fiction]] set in an [[anachronistic]] 19th-[[century]] [[society]].
#* {{quote-journal|author=James Blaylock|authorlink=James Blaylock|magazine=[[w:Locus (magazine)|Locus]]|month=May|year=1987|volume=20|issue=5 (#316 overall)|page=57|passage=There's railroad trains, a lot of steam-driven stuff, but that's about it. More ‘'''steam punk'''’, I suppose.}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=Ruth La Ferla|title=Steampunk moves between 2 worlds|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2008/05/08/fashion/08PUNK.html|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|date=May 8, 2008|passage=It is also the vision of '''steampunk''', a subculture that is the aesthetic expression of a time-traveling fantasy world, one that embraces music, film, design and now fashion, all inspired by the extravagantly inventive age of dirigibles and steam locomotives, brass diving bells and jar-shaped protosubmarines.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[writer]] of steampunk [[fiction]].
#* {{quote-journal|author=Kevin Wayne Jeter|authorlink=K. W. Jeter|title=[Untitled letter]|magazine=Locus|url=http://www.lettersofnote.com/2011/03/birth-of-steampunk.html|month=April|year=1987|volume=20|issue=4 (#315 overall)|page=57|passage=Personally, I think Victorian fantasies are going to be the next big thing, as long as we can come up with a fitting collective term for Powers, [[w:James Blaylock|[James] Blaylock]] and myself. Something based on the appropriate technology of the era; like '''steam-punks''', perhaps...}}
# {{lb|en|countable|cosplay}} A person [[cosplay]]ing as a steampunk character.
#* {{quote-journal|author=Klaude Davenport|title=An interview with Emmett and Klaude Davenport of the Clockwork Cabaret|url=http://efanzines.com/ExhibHall/ExhibHall-01.pdf|magazine=Exhibition Hall|month=September|year=2009|issue=1|page=6|passage=It wound up being an overwhelmingly positive experience that made me appreciate the '''steampunks''' around me even more.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|author=John Naylor|title=Re: [Steam-Scholars] Hello again and a query|id=45b85.6b512dc6.39cddc00@aol.com|newsgroup=steam-scholars|url=https://groups.google.com/forum/#!msg/steam-scholars/bdMTIoChWyQ/pz5TAUpNxcYJ|date=24 September 2010|passage=It is extremely rare that you speak to someone who says "I want to be an ...." This would suggest that for the vast majority of '''steampunks''' their choice of outfit (at least intitially) is less a conscious attempt at portrayal and more of a spontaneous and potentially subconscious growth of an idea.}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To depict in a steampunk manner.
#* {{quote-journal|author=John Lui|title=Musketeers victim of identity crisis [review of ''[[w:The Three Musketeers (2011 film)|The Three Musketeers]]'' (2011)]|newspaper={{w|The Straits Times}} (Life! section)|city=Singapore|date=26 October 2011|passage=[[w:Paul W. S. Anderson|[Director Paul W.S.] Anderson]]'s answer to the question of what to update in this film seems to be: '''steampunk''' everything. Hence the elaborate airship contraptions and weapons, all made in wood and iron and powered by choo-choo engines. What seems to be missing is the why. When far-fetched techno-bits and bobs are put into a story, these items must have a meaning and purpose. Here, the gadgets are throwaway items used for their visual effect, then discarded.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Sybil Fogg|chapter=Mechomancy: Steampunk Sensibilities in Pagan Traditions|title=Llewellyn's 2013 Magical Almanac: Practical Magic for Everyday Living|location=Woodbury, Minn.|publisher={{w|Llewellyn Worldwide}}|year=2012|page=90|isbn=978-0-7387-1515-5|passage=There is also a strong draw on literature and film for ideas. Some steampunks will take a favorite character, such as {{w|Boba Fett}}, Alice, Dorothy, [[w:Severus Snape|Professor Snape]], or {{w|Sherlock Holmes}}, and “'''steampunk'''” him or her out by adding elements of leather (or faux leather), gears, clock parts, electricity, motors, and so on.}}

